{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-hyperbolic.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Hyperbolic functions: the other circle",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the functions that walk the hyperbola $x^2 - y^2 = 1$ as $\\cos$ and $\\sin$ walk the circle, and read their derivatives off that picture.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The circle $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ is walked by $(\\cos t, \\sin t)$. Flip one sign and $x^2 - y^2 = 1$ is a hyperbola. What walks that?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Factor the hyperbola",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Look again at how the circle was walked. Over $\\mathbb{C}$ its equation factors, $x^2 + y^2 = (x + iy)(x - iy)$, and the point $x + iy = e^{it}$ makes the other factor $x - iy = e^{-it}$, so the product is $1$. The hyperbola's equation factors with no $i$ at all: $x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Invent it: copy the circle's move without the $i$, and set $x + y = e^t$. What must $x - y$ be for the product to be $1$? Solve the two equations for $x$ and $y$ before reading on.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$x - y = e^{-t}$; adding and subtracting the two equations gives $x = \\frac{e^t + e^{-t}}{2}$ and $y = \\frac{e^t - e^{-t}}{2}$. Any positive number in place of $e^t$ would give a point of the right branch. $e^t$ is the circle's choice with the $i$ taken out, and the rest of the lesson shows what it buys: $t$ measures the same thing on both curves, and the derivatives come out as simply as $\\cos' = -\\sin$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Hyperbolic cosine and sine\n> $\\cosh x = \\dfrac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}$, the average of $e^x$ and its mirror image $e^{-x}$; and $\\sinh x = \\dfrac{e^x - e^{-x}}{2}$, half their difference.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The engine reads `cosh`, `sinh` and `tanh` as these definitions in `exp`, so its answers come back in exponentials: everything about them is something about $e^x$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Even and odd parts of e^x",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from -1.39 to 1.4\nview -2, 2, -2.2, 4.6\nEp = graph(exp(x), x, -1.5, 1.5) faint color 1\nEm = graph(exp(-x), x, -1.5, 1.5) faint color 2\nA = point(t + i*exp(t)) color 1\nB = point(t + i*exp(-t)) color 2\nAB = segment(A, B) dashed color 6\nMc = point(t + i*cosh(t)) thick color 3\nCg = graph(cosh(x), x, -1.4, t) thick color 3\nCf = graph(cosh(x), x, -1.4, 1.4) thick color 3\nD = segment(Mc, A) thick color 4\nMs = point(t + i*sinh(t)) thick color 4\nSf = graph(sinh(x), x, -1.4, 1.4) thick color 4\n> show Ep, Em | $e^x$ (orange) grows to the right; its mirror image $e^{-x}$ (blue) grows to the left.\n> show A, B, AB, Mc, Cg; play t to 1.4 in 5s | At every $x$, take the point halfway between them: their average, $\\cosh x$ (green). It is symmetric, an **even** function, lowest at $\\cosh 0 = 1$.\n> hide Cg; show Cf, D | From the average up to $e^x$ is half their difference: $\\sinh x$ (pink). So $e^x = \\cosh x + \\sinh x$.\n> show Ms, Sf | Drawn on its own, $\\sinh x$ is **odd**: through $0$, and turned half a turn about it.\n> play t to -1.39 in 5s | Walk back. At every $x$, $e^x = \\cosh x + \\sinh x$ and $e^{-x} = \\cosh x - \\sinh x$: an even part and an odd part, the way every function splits.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cosh(x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cosh(x) + sinh(x))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cosh(x) - sinh(x))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cosh(-x) - cosh(x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The hyperbola",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$(\\cos t, \\sin t)$ stays on the circle because $\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1$. The hyperbolic pair was built to satisfy the same equation with one sign changed; multiplied out, the definitions confirm it:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cosh(x)^2 - sinh(x)^2)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] The unit hyperbola\n> $\\cosh^2 t - \\sinh^2 t = 1$: the point $(\\cosh t, \\sinh t)$ lies on the hyperbola $x^2 - y^2 = 1$, on its right branch, since $\\cosh t > 0$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1.4\nview -1.7, 2.7, -1.5, 2.45\nC = curve(exp(i*s), s, 0, 2pi) faint color 6\nH = curve(cosh(s) + i*sinh(s), s, -1.5, 1.5) color 3\nQ = point(exp(i*t)) thick color 1\nRQ = segment(0, Q) color 1\nP = point(cosh(t) + i*sinh(t)) thick color 3\nR = segment(0, P) color 3\nX = point(cosh(t)) color 2\nDX = segment(P, X) dashed color 2\nY = point(i*sinh(t)) color 4\nDY = segment(P, Y) dashed color 4\nV = value(cosh(t)^2 - sinh(t)^2, \"\\cosh^2 t - \\sinh^2 t = \")\nSC = poly(0, exp(i*0*t/12), exp(i*1*t/12), exp(i*2*t/12), exp(i*3*t/12), exp(i*4*t/12), exp(i*5*t/12), exp(i*6*t/12), exp(i*7*t/12), exp(i*8*t/12), exp(i*9*t/12), exp(i*10*t/12), exp(i*11*t/12), exp(i*12*t/12)) color 1\nSH = poly(0, cosh(0*t/12) + i*sinh(0*t/12), cosh(1*t/12) + i*sinh(1*t/12), cosh(2*t/12) + i*sinh(2*t/12), cosh(3*t/12) + i*sinh(3*t/12), cosh(4*t/12) + i*sinh(4*t/12), cosh(5*t/12) + i*sinh(5*t/12), cosh(6*t/12) + i*sinh(6*t/12), cosh(7*t/12) + i*sinh(7*t/12), cosh(8*t/12) + i*sinh(8*t/12), cosh(9*t/12) + i*sinh(9*t/12), cosh(10*t/12) + i*sinh(10*t/12), cosh(11*t/12) + i*sinh(11*t/12), cosh(12*t/12) + i*sinh(12*t/12)) color 3\nAr = value(t/2, \"\\text{each shaded area} = \")\n> show C, Q, RQ | The unit circle, $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, and $(\\cos t, \\sin t)$ on it.\n> show H, P, R | The unit hyperbola, $x^2 - y^2 = 1$, and $(\\cosh t, \\sinh t)$ on it: the same recipe with one sign flipped.\n> show X, DX, Y, DY, V; play t to 1.4 in 4s | The shadows of the green point are $\\cosh t$ (blue) and $\\sinh t$ (pink). The point runs out along the hyperbola, and $\\cosh^2 t - \\sinh^2 t$ never leaves $1$.\n> hide X, DX, Y, DY, V; play t to 0 in 2s | So what is $t$? On the circle it is an angle. On the hyperbola it is not: the green segment's angle never reaches $45°$, however large $t$ grows.\n> show SC, SH, Ar; play t to 1.4 in 5s | But on both, $t$ measures an **area**: the sector swept out from $1$ has area $\\frac{t}{2}$, on the circle and on the hyperbola alike. The hyperbola's sector is long and thin; its area grows at exactly the circle's rate.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why $\\frac t2$? A point $(x(s), y(s))$ moving from $s = 0$ to $t$ sweeps a sector of area $\\frac12 \\int_0^t (x y' - y x')\\,ds$. For the hyperbola the integrand is $\\cosh^2 s - \\sinh^2 s$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cosh(s)*diff(sinh(s), s) - sinh(s)*diff(cosh(s), s))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "integrate(1, s, 0, t)/2",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For the circle it is $\\cos^2 s + \\sin^2 s$, which is $1$ too, the circle's own equation (the engine leaves it written that way):",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cos(s)*diff(sin(s), s) - sin(s)*diff(cos(s), s))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Derivatives with no minus sign",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The circle's point $e^{it}$ has velocity $i e^{it}$: the radius turned a quarter, so $\\cos' = -\\sin$ and $\\sin' = \\cos$. Differentiate the hyperbola's point instead.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Predict first: differentiate $\\frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}$ term by term. Which function comes out, and with what sign? The cells check your answer by subtracting it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(cosh(x), x) - sinh(x)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(sinh(x), x) - cosh(x)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from -1.2 to 0.6\nview -1.4, 2.8, -1.8, 2.4\nH = curve(cosh(s) + i*sinh(s), s, -1.7, 1.7) faint color 3\nK = line(0, 1 + i) dashed color 6\nP = point(cosh(t) + i*sinh(t)) thick color 3\nR = arrow(0, P) color 3\nM = arrow(0, sinh(t) + i*cosh(t)) dashed color 4\nVel = arrow(P, cosh(t) + sinh(t) + i*(sinh(t) + cosh(t))) thick color 4\n> show H, P, R | The point $(\\cosh t, \\sinh t)$ and its position arrow.\n> show Vel; play t to 0.6 in 4s | Its velocity (pink) is $(\\sinh t, \\cosh t)$. The circle's velocity is its radius turned a quarter; this one is at right angles to the radius only at the vertex, $t = 0$.\n> show K, M | It is the position with $x$ and $y$ swapped: its mirror image in the line $y = x$ (dashed, from $0$). Reflecting has no minus sign, and neither do $\\cosh' = \\sinh$ and $\\sinh' = \\cosh$.\n> play t to -0.9 in 4s | The velocity's tip runs along that line: it is at $(\\cosh t + \\sinh t)(1, 1) = e^t (1, 1)$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The circle, turned by i",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!intuition] cos is cosh at an imaginary argument\n> Put $it$ into the definition: $\\cosh(it) = \\frac{e^{it} + e^{-it}}{2}$, and Euler's formula turns that into $\\cos t$; likewise $\\sinh(it) = i \\sin t$. Put $x = \\cos t$ and $y = i \\sin t$ into the hyperbola's $x^2 - y^2 = 1$ and it becomes the circle's $\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1$: the circle is the hyperbola with $y$ made imaginary.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "exptotrig(cosh(i*t))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(exptotrig(sinh(i*t)))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A chain hanging between two posts takes the shape of $\\cosh$, stretched to $a \\cosh(x/a)$ for some $a$: the **catenary**. Near the bottom $\\cosh x$ is almost the parabola $1 + \\frac{x^2}{2}$, and then it climbs away from it, exponentially:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "plot([cosh(x), 1 + x^2/2], x, -3, 3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "diff(cosh(3x), x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Differentiate $\\cosh(3x)$.",
      "hints": [
        "$\\cosh' = \\sinh$, with no minus sign.",
        "The chain rule: times the inner derivative, $3$."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "sinh(x)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Invent it: $\\sin$ is the function with $f'' = -f$, $f(0) = 0$ and $f'(0) = 1$. Which function has $f'' = f$, $f(0) = 0$ and $f'(0) = 1$?",
      "hints": [
        "Take the minus sign away: look among $\\cosh$ and $\\sinh$, whose derivatives have none.",
        "$\\sinh 0 = 0$ and $\\sinh' 0 = \\cosh 0 = 1$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "cosh(0)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "What is $\\cosh 0$, the $x$-coordinate of the point $t = 0$ on the hyperbola?",
      "hints": [
        "$e^0 = 1$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "expand(cosh(2x) - (cosh(x)^2 + sinh(x)^2))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Check the double-angle formula $\\cosh 2x = \\cosh^2 x + \\sinh^2 x$: what is the difference of the two sides?",
      "hints": [
        "Write everything in $e^{x}$ and expand."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Factor $x^2 - y^2$ as the circle's equation factors over $\\mathbb{C}$, and $\\cosh$ and $\\sinh$ fall out: the even and odd parts of $e^x$. The point $(\\cosh t, \\sinh t)$ walks the hyperbola $x^2 - y^2 = 1$ as $(\\cos t, \\sin t)$ walks the circle, with $t$ twice the area swept on both; its velocity is its mirror image, so $\\cosh' = \\sinh$ and $\\sinh' = \\cosh$; and $\\cosh(it) = \\cos t$, $\\sinh(it) = i \\sin t$. Next, back on the real line: how well does a tangent line stand in for its curve, and what does the derivative of the derivative say about the curve's shape?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
